FS(5)                          P32W Unix                           FS(5)

NAME
       fs - format of the disk and the file system volume

DESCRIPTION
       The disk. The CompactFlash card is read in 512-byte sectors:

              sector 0       the boot header
              sectors 1..    the kernel image (the boot area)
              sector 2048    the filesystem (by default; 1 MiB of
                             boot area)

       The boot header holds five words (32-bit, little-endian,
       like every number here): the magic 0x42323350 ("P32B"), the
       kernel's length in sectors, its load address, its entry
       point (both 0x00100000), and the sector where the filesystem
       starts. The monitor's b command uses the first four (see
       rc(8)); the kernel uses the fifth, or 2048 when the header
       has none. /dev/disk is the filesystem area (block n is
       sectors start + 2n and start + 2n + 1); /dev/rdisk is the
       whole card, raw. A disk image is built on the host by
       tools/p32fs.py.

       The filesystem is made of 1024-byte blocks, numbered from
       its start:

              block 0        the superblock
              blocks 1..     the inode table
              then           the free-block bitmap
              then           the data blocks, to nblocks - 1

       The superblock. Nine words:

              magic          0x50333246
              nblocks        blocks in the filesystem
              ninodes        inodes, a multiple of 16
              inode_start    the inode table's first block (1)
              bmap_start     the bitmap's first block
              data_start     the first data block
              clean          1 if cleanly unmounted
              free_blocks    free data blocks
              free_inodes    free inodes

       The kernel sets clean to 0 while the filesystem is mounted
       (and at each sync) and to 1 when it is unmounted, halted or
       rebooted; at boot or mount it warns when it finds 0.

       Inodes. 64 bytes each, 16 to a block; inode n is entry
       n mod 16 of block inode_start + n/16. Inode 0 is never used;
       inode 1 is the root directory. An inode is free when its
       mode is 0 (there is no free list: the kernel takes the first
       free one).

              struct dinode {
                  ushort mode, nlink, uid, gid;
                  uint size, mtime, ctime;
                  uint addr[11];
              };

       mode is the type, 0170000: 0100000 file, 040000 directory,
       020000 character device, 060000 block device, 010000 pipe;
       then 04000 set-user-id, 02000 set-group-id, 01000 sticky,
       and the 0777 permissions. nlink counts the directory entries
       naming the inode. There is no access time: stat(2) reports
       mtime for it. addr[0..7] are the first 8 data blocks;
       addr[8] a single-indirect block of 256 block numbers,
       addr[9] double- and addr[10] triple-indirect. A block number
       0 is a hole and reads as zeros. A device file keeps
       major << 8 | minor in addr[0].

       The bitmap. Bit b (byte b/8, bit b mod 8, low bit first) is
       1 when block b is in use; the blocks before data_start are
       always marked. One bitmap block covers 8192 blocks. The
       kernel looks for a free block starting after the one it
       allocated last, and clears each new block.

       Directories. A directory is a file of 32-byte entries:

              uint ino;          0: an empty slot
              char name[28];     NUL-padded

       A name has 1 to 28 characters (one of 28 has no NUL); a
       longer one gives ENAMETOOLONG. Every directory holds . and
       ..; the root's .. is itself.

       Sizes. mkfs(8) gives one inode per 8 blocks (64 at least).
       The 16 MiB card image has 15360 blocks and 1920 inodes; the
       RAM disk /dev/ram has 256 blocks. Sizes are 32-bit, so a
       file holds at most 4 GiB - 1 bytes.

       Devices. The device files made in /dev, with their major
       and minor numbers:

              /dev/disk   block 0, 0    the CF card's filesystem
              /dev/ram    block 1, 0    the RAM disk
              /dev/tty0   char 1, 0     terminal 0 (the console)
              /dev/tty1   char 1, 1     terminal 1
              /dev/null   char 2, 0     discards; reads end of file
              /dev/mem    char 2, 1     physical memory
              /dev/port   char 2, 2     the I/O card's ports
              /dev/rdisk  char 3, 0     the whole card, raw
              /dev/tty    char 4, 0     the process's own terminal

SEE ALSO
       mkfs(8), fsck(8), mount(8), df(1), stat(2), rc(8)

BUGS
       There is one bitmap and no cylinder groups, no block
       fragments, no symbolic links and no access times. A
       filesystem cannot be grown. Up to three filesystems can be
       mounted besides the root.
